          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does COPD guidance describe about when to add an inhaled corticosteroid?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve describes when an inhaled corticosteroid (a steroid medicine breathed in to calm airway inflammation) may be added in COPD, but it does not give a numerical cut-off.\n\n**What the guidance says:** The Global respiratory strategy report (educational summary) says that for people with a significant history of flare-ups (exacerbations), a long-acting bronchodilator (an airway-opening medicine) is the base treatment. An inhaled corticosteroid is added where the blood eosinophil count is high (eosinophils are a type of white blood cell measured by a routine blood test), or where features of coexisting asthma are present. The same report explains that the blood count is used because inhaled corticosteroids raise the risk of pneumonia, so the benefit needs to be identifiable for the treatment to be worth the risk.\n\n**What I could not find:** The retrieved passages do not give the eosinophil number that counts as \"high,\" or define how many flare-ups make a history \"significant.\" I have not filled those gaps from memory. A clinician or the full guideline text would give those figures.\n\n**Where authorities differ:** My search did not retrieve a COPD comparison between guidance bodies, and the COPD guideline passage has no disagreement section. The disagreement I retrieved concerns asthma reliever choice, which is a different question. So I cannot report a COPD-specific disagreement from these sources.\n\n**How strong the evidence is:** The retrieved passages do not rate the strength of evidence behind this recommendation, so I cannot say how firm it is.\n\nFor a personal decision, a clinician who knows your blood test results and flare-up history is the right person to ask. Useful questions to bring: \"What is my eosinophil count, and does it meet the threshold in the guideline you use?\" and \"How many flare-ups in the past year count as significant for me?\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
